#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# scripts/mutate — run a mutation battery, and refuse to run one against an uncommitted tree.
#
# Why this exists, stated plainly because five sessions have already paid for it. A mutation
# battery applies a mutant, runs the suite, and puts the file back. Every hand-rolled battery in
# this repository put the file back with `git checkout -- <file>`, which restores from HEAD — so
# run against a tree that still holds uncommitted work, that step deletes the work instead of the
# mutation.
#
# It has happened five times: SONNY-40, SONNY-64 and SONNY-83, each written up in this
# repository's changelog; SONNY-161, in its own closing comment; and PR #67's cycle-3 fix round.
# Three of those did something worse than cost a redo — they produced false measurements — and
# they did not all mislead in the same direction, which is the part worth getting right:
#
#   * SONNY-64's three bogus "kills" are the dangerous kind. A false kill says the suite covers
#     something it does not, so the battery hands back a reassurance nobody earned and the reader
#     stops looking. Once, so far.
#   * SONNY-161's five and SONNY-83's one were false *survivors*, against code that had just been
#     deleted. A survivor is an alarm — this script exits 2 for one — so those made coverage look
#     worse than it was and got chased. Costly, but they announce themselves.
#
# Both kinds were caught by luck rather than by design, and a battery whose whole value is that its
# result can be trusted is worth less than nothing when it lies comfortably. (PR #70 review, F9:
# this comment previously said "twice ... in the reassuring direction" and counted a false survivor
# as one of the two.)
#
# Every occurrence was answered with documentation, and the last happened in a session that had
# already written up an earlier one. So the answer here is mechanical rather than written: this
# script refuses to do anything at all while `git status --porcelain` prints a line, and it has
# no override flag. `scripts/mutate selftest` is the proof that the refusal fires — it stands up a
# throwaway repository, dirties it, runs this harness against it, and asserts both that the run
# was refused and that the uncommitted bytes are still there afterwards.
#
# The second route to a false measurement, closed by SONNY-176, found by PR #73's fix round on
# 2026-08-19: a session started a build while a battery was mid-mutant, and the two shared .build/.
# A battery's whole output is which mutants were KILLED and which SURVIVED, read off compile and
# test results, so anything else compiling into the same build directory changes what is being
# read. A shared llbuild database or module cache surfaces as a transient compile failure, which
# this harness records as `KILLED (build failure)` — a false kill, the reassuring direction again,
# and one nothing in the report can show: a contaminated run prints the same counts a clean one
# does.
#
# Two mechanisms, because the two halves of that are enforceable in different ways:
#
#   * A run lock, kept in .git/ so it can never dirty the tree, held for the battery's whole
#     duration. A second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout is refused, naming the run that holds
#     it. It is taken BEFORE the dirty-tree guard on purpose: a battery that is mid-mutant has a
#     dirty tree by construction, so a second run reaching that guard first would be refused for
#     the wrong reason and told to "commit or stash the paths above" — and the path above would be
#     a mutant.
#   * A build directory private to the battery (--scratch-path, with the clang module cache inside
#     it). Nothing a shell script does can stop another terminal from running `swift build`, so
#     this stops that build from mattering instead: it writes .build/, the battery writes its own
#     scratch directory, and neither can corrupt the other's artifacts.
#
# What is left over after those two is written out in `--help`, under "What this does and does not
# prevent", rather than implied away.
#
# Usage
#   scripts/mutate <plan>                run every mutant in the plan
#   scripts/mutate <plan> --only M3,M7   run only these mutants
#   scripts/mutate <plan> --check        parse the plan and match every mutant; run no tests
#   scripts/mutate selftest              exercise this harness against a throwaway repository
#   scripts/mutate unlock                clear a run lock left behind by a killed battery
#   scripts/mutate --help                the plan format, with an example
#
# Exit codes
#   0  the battery ran and every mutant was killed
#   2  the battery ran and at least one mutant SURVIVED — a survivor is a finding, not an error
#   1  the battery did not run, or its result could not be trusted: a dirty tree, another battery
#      already holding this checkout, a suite command that would build into the shared .build/, an
#      unparseable plan, a mutant whose `from` text does not match exactly once, a red baseline, or
#      a tree left dirty by the suite itself

set -euo pipefail

SELF="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
readonly SELF
readonly SCRIPT_NAME="scripts/mutate"

# The exact flagged test command from CLAUDE.md, plus two flags that are only isolation. Plain
# `swift test` never gets as far as running here — CLAUDE.md's Commands section has which flag
# fixes which failure — and the flagged command's own CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH and default build
# directory both sit in .build/, which every other build in this checkout also writes. Single quotes are deliberate — $MUTATE_SCRATCH is expanded by the subshell that runs
# the command, not now.
#
# Isolation does not change what is measured, which was checked rather than assumed: at c3b8166
# the flagged command and this one each reported 1410 tests in 111 suites, and a cold build of
# each carried the same six compiler-warning sites.
readonly DEFAULT_TEST_CMD='env CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH="$MUTATE_SCRATCH/clang-module-cache" swift test --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" --disable-sandbox -Xswiftc -F -Xswiftc /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/Frameworks -Xlinker -rpath -Xlinker /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/Frameworks -Xlinker -rpath -Xlinker /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/Library/Developer/usr/lib'

TEST_CMD="${MUTATE_TEST_CMD:-$DEFAULT_TEST_CMD}"
readonly TEST_CMD

WORKDIR=""

# The file currently carrying a mutant, and the byte-exact copy taken before it was applied. Set
# for exactly as long as the tree is mutated, so an interrupted run — Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, a die()
# from anywhere — puts the file back instead of leaving a mutant behind for someone to commit.
ACTIVE_FILE=""
ACTIVE_BACKUP=""

# The run lock. LOCK_DIR is set as soon as this process knows where the lock lives — including in a
# process the lock REFUSED — so LOCK_OWNED, not LOCK_DIR, is what release_lock tests. A refused
# second invocation must never remove the lock the first one is still holding.
LOCK_DIR=""
LOCK_OWNED=""

# The build directory this battery compiles into, and the one-line description of it the report
# prints. Both are set by require_isolated_test_command, which is also what refuses a replacement
# suite command that would build into the shared .build/ instead.
MUTATE_SCRATCH=""
TEST_CMD_ISOLATION=""

# Set only by finish(), which is the one way this script is allowed to end. Everything else —
# `set -u` on an unbound variable, `set -e` on a failed command, a syntax error on a path never
# taken — reaches the EXIT trap with this still empty, and assert_intended_exit turns that into a
# non-zero exit.
#
# It exists because on bash 3.2, the shell this actually runs under, **the presence of an EXIT trap
# makes a `set -u` death exit 0**. Measured three ways: the same death with no trap exits 1; with a
# trap it exits 0; and the usual remedy, a trap doing `rc=$?; trap - EXIT; exit $rc`, still exits 0,
# because $? is already 0 by the time the trap runs. There is nothing to preserve — the status has
# to be manufactured, so a legitimate exit has to announce itself. (PR #70 review, F6. The earlier
# fix for this symptom changed the battery() call site instead, which governs a real but different
# hazard — see the note there — and left this one live.)
MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED=""

finish() {
  MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED="yes"
  exit "$1"
}

# Defined above cleanup on purpose: cleanup runs from the EXIT trap, which is armed near the top
# of this file, and a trap that calls a function defined further down would fail rather than clean
# up if the harness ever died in between.
release_lock() {
  if [[ -n "$LOCK_OWNED" && -n "$LOCK_DIR" && -d "$LOCK_DIR" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  fi
  LOCK_OWNED=""
}

cleanup() {
  if [[ -n "$ACTIVE_FILE" && -f "$ACTIVE_BACKUP" ]]; then
    cp "$ACTIVE_BACKUP" "$ACTIVE_FILE" 2>/dev/null || true
    printf '%s: interrupted mid-mutant — restored %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$ACTIVE_FILE" >&2
    ACTIVE_FILE=""
  fi
  if [[ -n "$WORKDIR" && -d "$WORKDIR" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$WORKDIR"
    WORKDIR=""
  fi
  # After the restore above, never before it: the lock is what keeps a second battery out, and the
  # tree has to be back to what HEAD says before that second battery can possibly start.
  release_lock
}

# Runs last in every EXIT trap, after the cleanup it must not pre-empt.
assert_intended_exit() {
  if [[ -z "$MUTATE_EXIT_INTENDED" ]]; then
    printf '%s: exiting 1 — the harness ended at a point that is not one of its exits.\n' \
      "$SCRIPT_NAME" >&2
    printf '%s: any output above this line is not a battery result. See the error above it.\n' \
      "$SCRIPT_NAME" >&2
    exit 1
  fi
}

trap 'cleanup; assert_intended_exit' EXIT
trap 'finish 130' INT TERM

die() {
  printf '%s: %s\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$*" >&2
  finish 1
}

require_command() {
  command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "required command not found: $1"
}

usage() {
  cat <<'USAGE'
Usage: scripts/mutate <plan> [--only ID[,ID...]] [--check]
       scripts/mutate selftest
       scripts/mutate unlock
       scripts/mutate --help

Runs a mutation battery against the COMMITTED tree: for each mutant it replaces an exact block
of text, runs the test suite, records which tests failed, and restores the file byte for byte.

It refuses to start while the working tree is dirty, and it refuses to start beside another
battery in the same checkout. Those two refusals are the point of the script, and no flag turns
either of them off.

Plan format — one record per mutant, directives at column 0:

    # a comment; blank lines are ignored too
    >>> mutant M1 the pending-approval guard removed
    >>> file Sources/MacAgentCore/AgentViewModel.swift
    >>> from
            guard !isAwaitingApproval else { return }
    >>> to
    >>> end

    >>> mutant M2 the morning boundary widened by an hour
    >>> file Sources/MacAgent/CommandCenterView.swift
    >>> from
            case 5..<12: period = "Morning"
    >>> to
            case 5..<13: period = "Morning"
    >>> end

  >>> mutant <id> <note>   The id is the first word and must be unique; the rest is a note,
                           echoed in the report so the summary reads like the changelog entry
                           it will become.
  >>> file <path>          Repository-root-relative.
  >>> from ... >>> to      The exact lines to replace, copied verbatim out of the file,
                           indentation included. Matched as literal bytes — never as a regex.
  >>> to ... >>> end       The lines to put in their place. An empty block deletes them.

The `from` text must occur EXACTLY ONCE in the file. Zero matches or two abort the whole run
before a single test executes: a mutant that did not apply produces a green suite that reads
exactly like a passing battery, which is how one round here reported four mis-patched mutants
as clean runs.

Keep the plan file outside the working tree. A plan committed nowhere is an untracked file, and
an untracked file is a dirty tree — the harness will refuse its own plan.

Concurrency, and the build directory:
  A battery holds a run lock for its whole duration, in .git/ where it cannot dirty the tree, so a
  second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout is refused instead of interleaving with the first. It
  also builds into a scratch directory of its own rather than the shared .build/, so an ordinary
  `swift build` from another terminal cannot corrupt what this run measures.

  `scripts/mutate unlock` clears a lock left behind by a killed run. It refuses while the owning
  process is still alive, so it cannot break a live battery — and once that process is gone, the
  same command clears what it left.

  The first battery in a checkout pays for one cold build of that private scratch directory
  (about a minute and a half in this repository). Every battery after it reuses the directory and
  builds incrementally, so the cost is paid once, not per run.

What this does and does not prevent:
  Prevented: a second `scripts/mutate` in this checkout; and any other build in this checkout
  corrupting this run's compiled artifacts, its llbuild database, or its clang module cache.

  Not prevented, and worth knowing rather than assuming otherwise:

    * Nothing here stops another terminal from running `swift build` mid-battery. That build can
      no longer hurt the battery, but the battery still hurts it: it compiles whatever is on disk
      at that moment, which during a run includes a mutant.
    * A process that WRITES a tracked file during a mutant's suite run is caught after that
      mutant, by the dirty-tree check between mutants, not while it runs. A `git checkout` in this
      checkout mid-build is the loud version and usually breaks the build outright with "input
      file ... was modified during the build".
    * SwiftPM's caches under ~ (dependency clones, the manifest cache) are shared by every build
      on this machine and are outside a script's reach.
    * The lock is per-worktree, deliberately: each worktree has its own .build/, so two worktrees
      running batteries at once share nothing and are not refused.
    * MUTATE_TEST_CMD replaces the suite command outright. If the replacement invokes SwiftPM it
      must carry --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" or the run is refused; if it invokes something
      else, this script cannot know what that something else shares.

Environment:
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD   Replaces the suite command. Add `--filter <Suite>` through it for a fast
                    re-run of one mutant, keeping --scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH" in it. The
                    report always prints the command it actually ran, so a pasted log can never
                    misrepresent what was measured.
  MUTATE_SCRATCH    Exported by the harness, not set by you: the build directory private to this
                    run. A replacement suite command that builds with SwiftPM has to use it.

Output:
  Per-mutant logs are written under .build/mutate/<sha>-<timestamp>/, because .build/ is
  gitignored here and the harness must not dirty the tree it just insisted on. That is checked,
  not assumed: a repository that does not ignore .build/ gets its logs in a temp directory.
USAGE
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The guard. Everything else in this file is convenience.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `-c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal`, not a bare `git status`: that config is honoured by
# --porcelain, so `git config status.showUntrackedFiles no` deletes the untracked half of this
# guard without anyone editing the script — an effective override in a script whose stated property
# is that it has none. (PR #70 review, F1.)
require_clean_tree() {
  local dirty
  dirty="$(git -c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal status --porcelain)"
  if [[ -z "$dirty" ]]; then
    return 0
  fi
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — the working tree is not clean.

$dirty

A battery's result is only meaningful if it can be attributed to a commit, and its revert step is
only safe if what it reverts to is what is committed. Uncommitted work makes both untrue: the run
measures a tree no SHA names, and any recovery by \`git checkout\` — which is how every battery in
this repository used to revert — deletes the work instead of the mutation. That has happened five
times, and three of those produced false measurements: once a bogus kill, which is the dangerous
direction because it claims coverage that is not there, and twice bogus survivors against code
that had just been deleted.

Commit or stash the paths above, then re-run. There is no override flag.
EOF
  finish 1
}

require_clean_tree_after() {
  local context="$1"
  local dirty
  dirty="$(git -c status.showUntrackedFiles=normal status --porcelain)"
  if [[ -z "$dirty" ]]; then
    return 0
  fi
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: aborting — the tree is dirty after $context.

$dirty

The battery stops here rather than carrying an unexplained change into the next mutant, which
would make every result after this one describe a tree nobody chose. Restore the paths above by
hand and work out what wrote them before re-running.
EOF
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The second guard: one battery at a time, in a build directory of its own.
#
# The lock lives inside .git/, which `git status` never reports, so taking it cannot dirty the tree
# the next check is about to insist is clean. .git/ is also per-worktree — `git rev-parse
# --absolute-git-dir` answers .git/worktrees/<name> inside a linked worktree — and per-worktree is
# exactly the right scope, because each worktree has its own .build/ and two of them running
# batteries at once share nothing.
#
# `mkdir` is the whole atomicity argument: on any POSIX filesystem it either creates the directory
# or fails because it exists, in one step, with no window between the test and the create.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

lock_field() {
  [[ -f "$LOCK_DIR/owner" ]] || return 0
  sed -n "s/^$1 //p" "$LOCK_DIR/owner" | head -1
}

# Prints why the lock is stale, or prints nothing at all if its owner is genuinely still running a
# battery. Both halves of a pid check matter here: a pid that no longer exists is the ordinary
# killed-run case, and a pid that exists but belongs to something else is pid reuse, which on a Mac
# that has wrapped around its pid space is not exotic. The one case this reads as live when it is
# not is a pid reused by anything whose command line happens to say "mutate" — another battery, or
# an editor with this file open. That error refuses a run rather than allowing two, and
# `scripts/mutate unlock` reports the same reading, so the way out of it is visible.
lock_stale_reason() {
  local pid="$1" owner_command
  if [[ ! -f "$LOCK_DIR/owner" ]]; then
    printf 'it carries no owner record, so whatever made it died between the mkdir and the write'
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ ! "$pid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    printf 'its owner record names no usable pid'
    return 0
  fi
  owner_command="$(ps -p "$pid" -o command= 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [[ -z "$owner_command" ]]; then
    printf 'the process that took it (pid %s) is gone' "$pid"
    return 0
  fi
  case "$owner_command" in
    *mutate*) return 0 ;;
  esac
  printf 'pid %s belongs to something else now: %s' "$pid" "$owner_command"
}

refuse_concurrent_run() {
  local pid="$1"
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — another battery already holds this checkout.

  pid     : ${pid:-unknown}
  started : $(lock_field started)
  plan    : $(lock_field plan)
  head    : $(lock_field head)
  lock    : $LOCK_DIR

Two batteries in one checkout share the working tree and the build directory. The tree already
carries the other run's mutant, and both runs compile out of the same directory, so each one is
measuring a tree and a set of artifacts the other is changing underneath it. Nothing in either
report could show that: a contaminated battery prints the same counts a clean one does, and the
comfortable direction to be wrong in is a false KILL, which claims coverage that is not there.

Wait for that run to finish. If it is already gone, \`$SCRIPT_NAME unlock\` clears the lock — it
refuses while the owner is alive, so it cannot break a live run.
EOF
  finish 1
}

acquire_lock() {
  local plan="$1" git_dir attempt reason owner_pid
  git_dir="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir 2>/dev/null)" \
    || die "not inside a git repository — there is nowhere to hold the run lock"
  LOCK_DIR="$git_dir/mutate.lock"

  for attempt in 1 2 3; do
    if mkdir "$LOCK_DIR" 2>/dev/null; then
      LOCK_OWNED="yes"
      {
        printf 'pid %s\n' "$$"
        printf 'started %s\n' "$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')"
        printf 'plan %s\n' "$plan"
        printf 'head %s\n' "$(git rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown')"
      } >"$LOCK_DIR/owner"
      return 0
    fi

    owner_pid="$(lock_field pid)"
    reason="$(lock_stale_reason "$owner_pid")"
    [[ -n "$reason" ]] || refuse_concurrent_run "$owner_pid"
    printf '%s: cleared a stale lock — %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$reason" >&2
    rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  done

  die "could not take the run lock at $LOCK_DIR — it is being created and cleared underneath this
       run. Something else is starting batteries in a loop; stop that before measuring anything."
}

# `scripts/mutate unlock`. Every state a lock can be left in has a way out that is not a hand-edit:
# gone owner or unreadable record, cleared here; live owner, refused here and cleared by this same
# command once that process ends, however it ends.
unlock_command() {
  require_command git
  local root git_dir pid reason
  root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  cd "$root"
  git_dir="$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  LOCK_DIR="$git_dir/mutate.lock"

  if [[ ! -d "$LOCK_DIR" ]]; then
    printf '%s: no run lock is held in this checkout.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
    printf '    looked at %s\n' "$LOCK_DIR"
    finish 0
  fi

  pid="$(lock_field pid)"
  reason="$(lock_stale_reason "$pid")"
  if [[ -z "$reason" ]]; then
    cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to clear the lock — pid $pid is still running a battery.

  started : $(lock_field started)
  plan    : $(lock_field plan)
  head    : $(lock_field head)

Clearing it now would let a second battery start beside that one, which is the measurement this
lock exists to prevent. End that run first (\`kill $pid\`) and its own trap releases the lock on the
way out. If it was killed with -9 and left this behind, run this command again — with the process
gone the lock is stale and this clears it.
EOF
    finish 1
  fi

  rm -rf -- "$LOCK_DIR"
  printf '%s: cleared a stale lock — %s.\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME" "$reason"
  finish 0
}

# Where this battery builds. Same shape and the same reason as the log directory below: .build/ is
# gitignored here, so a scratch directory inside it cannot dirty the tree, and a repository that
# does not ignore .build/ gets one outside the working tree instead.
mutate_scratch_path() {
  if git check-ignore -q ".build/mutate-scratch" 2>/dev/null; then
    printf '%s/.build/mutate-scratch' "$1"
  else
    printf '%s/scratch' "$WORKDIR"
  fi
}

# MUTATE_TEST_CMD replaces the suite command outright, which means it can replace the isolation
# with it. A replacement that invokes SwiftPM without a scratch path builds into the shared .build/
# — the exact hazard this file just spent two mechanisms on — so it is refused rather than warned
# about, because a warning in a report nobody re-reads is the non-mechanism this repository keeps
# rejecting. A replacement that is not a SwiftPM invocation at all has no build directory to share;
# the selftest's stand-ins are all of that kind, and the report says so rather than claiming an
# isolation that means nothing there.
#
# The word test is deliberately narrow: `swift` followed by whitespace and not preceded by a word
# character or a dot. `-Xswiftc` and `sample.swift` are both `swift` inside a longer token and
# neither is a SwiftPM invocation.
require_isolated_test_command() {
  case " $TEST_CMD " in
    *[!A-Za-z0-9_.]swift[[:space:]]*) ;;
    *)
      TEST_CMD_ISOLATION="not a SwiftPM invocation — it has no build directory to share"
      return 0
      ;;
  esac
  case "$TEST_CMD" in
    *--scratch-path*|*MUTATE_SCRATCH*)
      TEST_CMD_ISOLATION="$MUTATE_SCRATCH (this run's own, not the shared .build/)"
      return 0
      ;;
  esac
  cat >&2 <<EOF
$SCRIPT_NAME: refusing to run — MUTATE_TEST_CMD builds with SwiftPM and names no scratch path.

  $TEST_CMD

That builds into the same .build/ every other build in this checkout writes, and a shared build
directory is how a battery ends up reporting a result nothing in its output can show is wrong.
This run's own directory is exported as \$MUTATE_SCRATCH — put it in the command:

  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='swift test --scratch-path "\$MUTATE_SCRATCH" --filter SomeTests'

Single-quote it, so \$MUTATE_SCRATCH expands when the command runs rather than when you set it.
EOF
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plan parsing. Parallel arrays; index i is one mutant. The macOS system bash is 3.2, so no
# associative arrays. `from`/`to` blocks are held as files, not variables, because they are
# matched as raw bytes and a shell variable cannot hold a trailing newline honestly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

M_COUNT=0
M_ID=()
M_FILE=()
M_NOTE=()

parse_plan() {
  local plan="$1"
  [[ -f "$plan" ]] || die "plan file not found: $plan"

  local state="top" lineno=0 line rest existing i
  local id="" note="" file=""

  while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n "$line" ]]; do
    lineno=$((lineno + 1))
    case "$state" in
      top)
        if [[ -z "${line//[[:space:]]/}" || "$line" == '#'* ]]; then
          continue
        fi
        [[ "$line" == '>>> mutant '* ]] \
          || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> mutant <id> <note>', got: $line"
        rest="${line#>>> mutant }"
        id="${rest%%[[:space:]]*}"
        [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$ ]] \
          || die "$plan:$lineno: '$id' is not a usable mutant id (letters, digits, . _ - only)"
        i=0
        while [[ $i -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
          existing="${M_ID[$i]}"
          [[ "$existing" != "$id" ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: duplicate mutant id: $id"
          i=$((i + 1))
        done
        note="${rest#"$id"}"
        note="${note#"${note%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
        state="want-file"
        ;;
      want-file)
        [[ "$line" == '>>> file '* ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> file <path>', got: $line"
        file="${line#>>> file }"
        [[ -n "$file" ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: '>>> file' names no path"
        state="want-from"
        ;;
      want-from)
        [[ "$line" == '>>> from' ]] || die "$plan:$lineno: expected '>>> from', got: $line"
        : >"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from"
        state="in-from"
        ;;
      in-from)
        if [[ "$line" == '>>> to' ]]; then
          : >"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.to"
          state="in-to"
        else
          printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from"
        fi
        ;;
      in-to)
        if [[ "$line" == '>>> end' ]]; then
          [[ -s "$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.from" ]] \
            || die "$plan:$lineno: mutant $id has an empty '>>> from' block"
          M_ID[$M_COUNT]="$id"
          M_FILE[$M_COUNT]="$file"
          M_NOTE[$M_COUNT]="$note"
          M_COUNT=$((M_COUNT + 1))
          state="top"
        else
          printf '%s\n' "$line" >>"$WORKDIR/m$M_COUNT.to"
        fi
        ;;
    esac
  done <"$plan"

  [[ "$state" == "top" ]] \
    || die "$plan: unexpected end of file — mutant '$id' is missing its '>>> end'"
  [[ $M_COUNT -gt 0 ]] || die "$plan: contains no mutants"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Literal byte matching and patching. python3 in binary mode, deliberately: a battery here once
# used `perl -0pi` and corrupted UTF-8 punctuation across a file's doc comments, mutating far more
# than it meant to. Reading and writing bytes cannot re-encode anything it did not touch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

occurrences() {
  python3 - "$1" "$2" <<'PY'
import pathlib, sys
haystack = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_bytes()
needle = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).read_bytes()
print(haystack.count(needle))
PY
}

apply_mutant() {
  python3 - "$1" "$2" "$3" <<'PY'
import pathlib, sys
target = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1])
data = target.read_bytes()
needle = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[2]).read_bytes()
replacement = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[3]).read_bytes()
if data.count(needle) != 1:
    sys.stderr.write("match count changed between pre-flight and apply\n")
    sys.exit(1)
target.write_bytes(data.replace(needle, replacement, 1))
PY
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running and reading the suite. swift-testing's console glyphs vary with the terminal, so every
# pattern below matches the words, never the icon.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

run_suite() {
  local log="$1"
  local code=0
  bash -c "$TEST_CMD" >"$log" 2>&1 || code=$?
  return "$code"
}

suite_started() {
  grep -q 'Test run started\.' "$1"
}

run_tally() {
  # -E, not BRE: BSD sed has no \| alternation and would silently print nothing.
  sed -nE 's/^.*Test run with (.*) (passed|failed) after .*$/\1/p' "$1" | tail -1
}

# One line per failing test: "Test <name> failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue." A
# parameterized test reads "<name> with 10 test cases failed after ...", so the case count is
# trimmed off the end. The run-level line ("Test run with N tests ... failed") is excluded by
# name, and suite-level lines never contain " Test ".
failing_tests() {
  sed -n 's/^.*[[:space:]]Test \(.*\) failed after .*$/\1/p' "$1" \
    | grep -v '^run with ' \
    | sed 's/ with [0-9][0-9]* test cases$//' \
    | sort -u
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The battery
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

battery() {
  local plan="$1" only="$2" check_only="$3"

  require_command git
  require_command python3

  local root
  root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || die "not inside a git repository"
  cd "$root"

  # Before require_clean_tree, deliberately, and the order is load-bearing rather than tidy. A
  # battery that is mid-mutant has a dirty tree by construction, so a second run reaching the tree
  # guard first is refused with the wrong message: it prints the mutated file and says "commit or
  # stash the paths above". Telling a second session to commit a mutant is worse than not refusing
  # it at all.
  acquire_lock "$plan"

  MUTATE_SCRATCH="$(mutate_scratch_path "$root")"
  export MUTATE_SCRATCH
  require_isolated_test_command

  require_clean_tree

  local sha branch
  sha="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
  branch="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"

  parse_plan "$plan"

  # Selection: indices to run, in plan order. An unknown id is a typo, not an empty selection.
  local selected=() i j found wanted
  if [[ -z "$only" ]]; then
    i=0
    while [[ $i -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
      selected[${#selected[@]}]=$i
      i=$((i + 1))
    done
  else
    local saved_ifs="$IFS"
    IFS=','
    set -f
    local requested=($only)
    set +f
    IFS="$saved_ifs"
    [[ ${#requested[@]} -gt 0 ]] || die "--only was given an empty list"
    for wanted in "${requested[@]}"; do
      [[ -n "$wanted" ]] || continue
      found=""
      j=0
      while [[ $j -lt $M_COUNT ]]; do
        if [[ "${M_ID[$j]}" == "$wanted" ]]; then
          selected[${#selected[@]}]=$j
          found="yes"
        fi
        j=$((j + 1))
      done
      [[ -n "$found" ]] || die "--only names a mutant the plan does not define: $wanted"
    done
    [[ ${#selected[@]} -gt 0 ]] || die "--only selected no mutants"
  fi

  printf '==> %s\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
  printf '    repository : %s\n' "$root"
  printf '    HEAD       : %s (%s)\n' "$sha" "$branch"
  printf '    plan       : %s (%d of %d mutants selected)\n' "$plan" "${#selected[@]}" "$M_COUNT"
  printf '    test cmd   : %s\n' "$TEST_CMD"
  printf '    tree       : clean\n'
  printf '    lock       : held by this run (pid %s) at %s\n' "$$" "$LOCK_DIR"
  printf '    build dir  : %s\n' "$TEST_CMD_ISOLATION"
  printf '\n'

  # Pre-flight before anything runs: every selected mutant must match exactly once. Reported for
  # all of them at once, because fixing a plan one abort at a time is how a session gives up on
  # the plan and hand-rolls a battery again.
  printf '==> Pre-flight: matching every mutant against the tree\n'
  local mismatches=0 count idx
  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    if [[ ! -f "${M_FILE[$idx]}" ]]; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  NO SUCH FILE\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
      continue
    fi
    # Tracked, or the run means nothing. `>>> file ../outside.txt` used to pass pre-flight and be
    # mutated and restored happily, with "Summary at <sha>" printed over a file the SHA does not
    # describe — and `git status` can never see it, so an interrupted run would leave that mutant
    # somewhere the guard will never flag. A gitignored path inside the tree has the same two
    # problems. One check closes both. (PR #70 review, F2.)
    if ! git ls-files --error-unmatch -- "${M_FILE[$idx]}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  NOT TRACKED BY GIT — REFUSED\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
      continue
    fi
    count="$(occurrences "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.from")"
    if [[ "$count" == "1" ]]; then
      printf '    %-6s %s  1 match\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    else
      printf '    %-6s %s  %s matches — REFUSED\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$count"
      mismatches=$((mismatches + 1))
    fi
  done
  if [[ $mismatches -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    die "$mismatches mutant(s) were refused. Nothing was run and nothing was changed.
       A mutant that does not apply runs an unmutated suite and reports a clean pass, so a bad
       match aborts instead — copy the \`from\` block out of the file again, indentation included.
       A file git does not track cannot be attributed to the SHA this run would print, so it is
       refused too; \`>>> file\` paths are repository-root-relative and must be tracked."
  fi
  printf '\n'

  if [[ -n "$check_only" ]]; then
    printf '==> --check: every selected mutant matches. No tests were run, nothing was changed.\n'
    printf '    This is not a battery result. Re-run without --check to measure anything.\n'
    finish 0
  fi

  # Logs go under .build/, which this repository gitignores — writing them anywhere tracked
  # would dirty the tree the script just insisted was clean, and abort its own next mutant. The
  # property is checked rather than assumed, and a repository that does not ignore .build/ gets
  # its logs outside the working tree instead.
  local stamp logdir
  stamp="$(date +%Y%m%dT%H%M%S)"
  if git check-ignore -q ".build/mutate" 2>/dev/null; then
    logdir=".build/mutate/${sha:0:7}-$stamp"
    mkdir -p "$logdir"
  else
    logdir="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate-logs.XXXXXX")"
    printf '==> .build/ is not gitignored here, so logs go outside the tree:\n    %s\n\n' "$logdir"
  fi

  # A red baseline makes every "kill" below meaningless — the tests were already failing. This is
  # the same class of false measurement the dirty-tree guard exists to stop, so it is not optional.
  printf '==> Baseline: the unmutated tree\n'
  local started elapsed baseline_code=0
  started=$SECONDS
  run_suite "$logdir/baseline.log" || baseline_code=$?
  elapsed=$((SECONDS - started))
  if [[ $baseline_code -ne 0 ]]; then
    printf '    BASELINE FAILED after %ds — see %s\n' "$elapsed" "$logdir/baseline.log"
    printf '\n'
    failing_tests "$logdir/baseline.log" | sed 's/^/      /'
    printf '\n'
    die "the suite is red before any mutant was applied, so no kill this battery reported could
       be attributed to a mutant. Fix the suite, then re-run."
  fi
  printf '    PASSED  %s  (%ds)\n\n' "$(run_tally "$logdir/baseline.log")" "$elapsed"

  local killed=0 survived=0
  local survivor_ids=() outcomes=()
  local backup log code names name_count outcome

  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    printf '==> %s  %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
    printf '    %s\n' "${M_FILE[$idx]}"

    backup="$WORKDIR/backup.$idx"
    cp "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$backup"
    ACTIVE_FILE="${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    ACTIVE_BACKUP="$backup"
    if ! apply_mutant "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.from" "$WORKDIR/m$idx.to"; then
      cp "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
      ACTIVE_FILE=""
      die "failed to apply ${M_ID[$idx]} — ${M_FILE[$idx]} was restored from its backup"
    fi

    # Fault-injection seam, set only by `selftest`: reference a genuinely unbound variable so
    # `set -u` kills the script HERE — with the mutant in the file and ACTIVE_FILE set. That
    # placement is the whole point of it. It used to sit before the baseline, where a death leaves
    # cleanup with nothing to restore, so the selftest passed just as happily with
    # `assert_intended_exit` running *ahead* of `cleanup` — the one ordering that abandons the
    # mutant in the tree. Injected here, the ordering is observable and the selftest fails when it
    # is wrong. Do not move it earlier. (PR #70 cycle-3 re-check, G1.)
    if [[ -n "${MUTATE_INJECT_INTERNAL_ERROR:-}" ]]; then
      printf '    (selftest: injecting an unbound-variable death with the mutant applied)\n'
      printf '%s\n' "$MUTATE_A_VARIABLE_THAT_IS_DELIBERATELY_NEVER_SET"
    fi

    log="$logdir/${M_ID[$idx]}.log"
    code=0
    started=$SECONDS
    run_suite "$log" || code=$?
    elapsed=$((SECONDS - started))

    # Restore from the byte-exact copy taken above, not from HEAD. The tree was verified clean
    # before the run, so the two are the same thing here — but a revert that cannot reach past the
    # file it mutated is a revert that cannot destroy anything else either.
    cp "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}"
    cmp -s "$backup" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" || die "failed to restore ${M_FILE[$idx]} from its backup"
    ACTIVE_FILE=""
    require_clean_tree_after "${M_ID[$idx]}"

    if [[ $code -eq 0 ]]; then
      outcome="SURVIVED"
      survived=$((survived + 1))
      survivor_ids[${#survivor_ids[@]}]="$idx"
      printf '    SURVIVED — the whole suite passed with this mutant in place  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      printf '    A survivor is a finding: nothing in the suite holds this behaviour.\n'
    elif ! suite_started "$log"; then
      outcome="KILLED (build failure)"
      killed=$((killed + 1))
      printf '    KILLED by the compiler — the suite never ran  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
      printf '    No test evidence: this says the mutant does not build, not that it is covered.\n'
    else
      names="$(failing_tests "$log")"
      if [[ -z "$names" ]]; then
        outcome="KILLED (no named tests)"
        killed=$((killed + 1))
        printf '    KILLED — the run failed but named no test  (%ds)\n' "$elapsed"
        printf '    Read %s; a crash or a harness error looks like this.\n' "$log"
      else
        name_count="$(printf '%s\n' "$names" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')"
        outcome="KILLED by $name_count test(s)"
        killed=$((killed + 1))
        printf '    KILLED by %s test(s)  (%ds)\n' "$name_count" "$elapsed"
        printf '%s\n' "$names" | sed 's/^/      /'
      fi
    fi
    outcomes[$idx]="$outcome"
    printf '\n'
  done

  printf '==> Summary at %s\n' "$sha"
  printf '    %d mutant(s): %d killed, %d survived\n' "${#selected[@]}" "$killed" "$survived"
  printf '    logs: %s\n' "$logdir"
  printf '\n'
  for idx in "${selected[@]}"; do
    printf '    %-6s %-24s %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${outcomes[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
  done
  if [[ $survived -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf '\n'
    printf '    SURVIVORS — each one is a coverage finding, not a harness error:\n'
    for idx in "${survivor_ids[@]}"; do
      printf '      %-6s %s  %s\n' "${M_ID[$idx]}" "${M_FILE[$idx]}" "${M_NOTE[$idx]}"
    done
    printf '\n'
    printf '    Exiting 2 because of them. Every measurement above was taken at %s.\n' "$sha"
    finish 2
  fi
  printf '\n'
  printf '    Every mutant was killed. Measured at %s.\n' "$sha"
  finish 0
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# selftest — the acceptance criterion for the guard above.
#
# A guard nobody has watched fire is exactly the class of claim this repository distrusts
# everywhere else, and it is how the previous version of this abort silently stopped existing.
# So: stand up a throwaway git repository, dirty it on purpose, run this same script against it,
# and assert both that it refused and that the uncommitted bytes are still on disk afterwards.
# The real repository is never touched — everything happens under a mktemp directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

SELFTEST_ROOT=""
SELFTEST_FAILURES=0
ST_OUT=""
ST_EXIT=0

selftest_cleanup() {
  if [[ -n "$SELFTEST_ROOT" && -d "$SELFTEST_ROOT" ]]; then
    rm -rf -- "$SELFTEST_ROOT"
  fi
}

st_pass() { printf '    PASS  %s\n' "$1"; }

st_fail() {
  printf '    FAIL  %s\n' "$1" >&2
  SELFTEST_FAILURES=$((SELFTEST_FAILURES + 1))
}

st_eq() {
  if [[ "$2" == "$3" ]]; then st_pass "$1"; else st_fail "$1 — expected [$3], got [$2]"; fi
}

st_contains() {
  case "$2" in
    *"$3"*) st_pass "$1" ;;
    *) st_fail "$1 — output did not contain [$3]" ;;
  esac
}

st_absent() {
  case "$2" in
    *"$3"*) st_fail "$1 — output unexpectedly contained [$3]" ;;
    *) st_pass "$1" ;;
  esac
}

# Runs the real script, with a stand-in for the Swift suite so a selftest needs no toolchain.
st_run() {
  local cmd="$1"
  shift
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$cmd" "$SELF" "$@" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
}

selftest() {
  require_command git
  require_command python3

  SELFTEST_ROOT="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate-selftest.XXXXXX")"
  trap 'cleanup; selftest_cleanup; assert_intended_exit' EXIT
  trap 'finish 130' INT TERM

  local root="$SELFTEST_ROOT"
  local sentinel="$root/the-suite-ran.marker"
  mkdir -p "$root/repo"
  cd "$root/repo"

  git -c init.defaultBranch=main init -q .
  git config user.email "mutate-selftest@example.invalid"
  git config user.name "mutate selftest"
  git config commit.gpgsign false

  cat >sample.swift <<'SAMPLE'
enum Period {
    static func label(hour: Int) -> String {
        if hour < 12 { return "Morning" }
        return "Afternoon"
    }

    static func fallback() -> String {
        return "Afternoon"
    }
}
SAMPLE
  git add sample.swift
  git commit -q -m "seed"
  cp sample.swift "$root/sample.pristine"
  local seeded_sha
  seeded_sha="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"

  # Plans live outside the repository: a plan file inside it would itself make the tree dirty.
  cat >"$root/kill.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 the morning boundary widened by an hour
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        if hour < 12 { return "Morning" }
>>> to
        if hour < 13 { return "Morning" }
>>> end
PLAN

  cat >"$root/nomatch.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 a mutant whose text is not in the file
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        if hour < 99 { return "Nowhere" }
>>> to
        if hour < 98 { return "Nowhere" }
>>> end
PLAN

  cat >"$root/ambiguous.plan" <<'PLAN'
>>> mutant M1 a mutant whose text appears twice
>>> file sample.swift
>>> from
        return "Afternoon"
>>> to
        return "Evening"
>>> end
PLAN

  # Stand-ins for the suite, shaped like swift-testing's console output so the report's parsing is
  # exercised too. `kills_the_mutant` fails only while the mutant is in the file, which is what a
  # real killing test does.
  local always_pass='printf "X  Test run started.\n"
printf "X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local kills_the_mutant='printf "X  Test run started.\n"
if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  printf "X  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  printf "X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
  exit 1
fi
printf "X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  # Signals the harness only once the mutant is in the file. Signalling unconditionally would
  # land during the baseline, before anything is mutated, and the restore assertion below would
  # then pass without the restore path ever running.
  local signals_the_harness='if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
  kill -TERM $PPID
fi
printf "X  Test run started.\n"
printf "X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n"'

  local always_fails='printf "X  Test run started.\n"
printf "X  Test somethingAlreadyBroken() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
printf "X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n"
exit 1'

  local records_that_it_ran="touch '$sentinel'
printf \"X  Test run started.\n\"
printf \"X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n\""

  printf '==> %s selftest\n' "$SCRIPT_NAME"
  printf '    throwaway repository: %s\n\n' "$root/repo"

  # Every check below runs with the sentinel stand-in, and asserts BOTH the wording only
  # require_clean_tree emits ("refusing to run") and that no suite ran. Without those two, this
  # group passes against a guard that has been deleted outright: require_clean_tree_after also
  # exits 1 and also prints the dirty path, just after both suites have already run against the
  # dirty tree. Measured — with require_clean_tree's git call replaced by an empty string, 2 of
  # this group's 4 checks and the whole untracked group still passed. (PR #70 review, F7.)
  printf '==> The guard: a dirty tree is refused, and the uncommitted work survives\n'
  printf 'let uncommittedWorkInProgress = true\n' >>sample.swift
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a dirty tree exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the refusal comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "the refusal says the tree is not clean" "$ST_OUT" "working tree is not clean"
  st_contains "the refusal prints the dirty path" "$ST_OUT" "sample.swift"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "a suite ran against the dirty tree — the refusal came too late to be this guard"
  else
    st_pass "no suite ran"
  fi
  if grep -q "uncommittedWorkInProgress" sample.swift; then
    st_pass "the uncommitted work is still on disk afterwards"
  else
    st_fail "the uncommitted work was destroyed — this is the bug the script exists to stop"
  fi
  git checkout -- sample.swift

  printf '\n==> The guard covers an untracked file too\n'
  printf 'scratch\n' >"$root/repo/scratch.txt"
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an untracked file exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the refusal comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "the refusal names the untracked file" "$ST_OUT" "scratch.txt"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi

  printf '\n==> status.showUntrackedFiles=no does not silently remove half the guard\n'
  # git status --porcelain honours that config, so setting it used to delete the untracked half
  # of the guard with no edit to this script at all. (PR #70 review, F1.)
  git config status.showUntrackedFiles no
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an untracked file still exits 1 with the config set" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and still comes from the pre-run guard" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "and still names the file" "$ST_OUT" "scratch.txt"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then st_fail "a suite ran anyway"; else st_pass "no suite ran"; fi
  git config --unset status.showUntrackedFiles
  rm -f "$root/repo/scratch.txt"

  printf '\n==> The guard covers --check as well — no command tolerates a dirty tree\n'
  printf 'let alsoUncommitted = true\n' >>sample.swift
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan" --check
  st_eq "--check on a dirty tree exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "--check refuses for the same reason" "$ST_OUT" "refusing to run"
  st_contains "--check names the dirty tree" "$ST_OUT" "working tree is not clean"
  git checkout -- sample.swift

  printf '\n==> A mutant whose text does not match is refused before any test runs\n'
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/nomatch.plan"
  st_eq "a non-matching mutant exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the report says how many times it matched" "$ST_OUT" "0 matches — REFUSED"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "the suite ran anyway — a mis-patched mutant would report a clean pass"
  else
    st_pass "the suite never ran"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A mutant whose text appears twice is refused as ambiguous\n'
  rm -f "$sentinel"
  st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/ambiguous.plan"
  st_eq "an ambiguous mutant exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the report says how many times it matched" "$ST_OUT" "2 matches — REFUSED"
  if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
    st_fail "the suite ran anyway"
  else
    st_pass "the suite never ran"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A red baseline aborts instead of attributing kills to mutants\n'
  st_run "$always_fails" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a red baseline exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the abort names the baseline" "$ST_OUT" "BASELINE FAILED"
  st_contains "and says why no kill could be attributed" "$ST_OUT" "red before any mutant was applied"

  printf '\n==> --only rejects an id the plan does not define\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan" --only NOPE
  st_eq "an unknown --only id exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "the error names the id" "$ST_OUT" "does not define: NOPE"

  printf '\n==> A killed mutant is reported as killed, with the tests that killed it\n'
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "an all-killed battery exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the kill is counted" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_contains "the killing test is named" "$ST_OUT" "labelIsMorningBeforeNoon()"
  st_absent "a kill is not reported as a survivor" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVED"
  st_contains "the report carries the SHA it measured at" "$ST_OUT" "$seeded_sha"

  printf '\n==> A survivor is loud, distinguishable, and exits non-zero\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a survivor exits 2" "$ST_EXIT" "2"
  st_contains "the survivor is called out" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVED"
  st_contains "and listed again in the summary" "$ST_OUT" "SURVIVORS"
  st_absent "a survivor is not reported as a kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED"

  printf '\n==> The default suite command — the one every real battery uses — is isolated\n'
  # Nothing else here can reach this line. Every check in this selftest replaces the suite
  # command with a stand-in, so the default is the one thing a real battery depends on that a
  # selftest never executes — and it is also the line an edit would most plausibly undo, by
  # pasting CLAUDE.md's flagged command back over it. Read as text, because that is what can be
  # checked without a Swift toolchain.
  local default_cmd
  default_cmd="$(sed -n 's/^readonly DEFAULT_TEST_CMD=//p' "$SELF")"
  st_contains "the default builds into the run's own scratch directory" \
    "$default_cmd" '--scratch-path "$MUTATE_SCRATCH"'
  st_contains "and keeps its clang module cache inside it" \
    "$default_cmd" 'CLANG_MODULE_CACHE_PATH="$MUTATE_SCRATCH/'
  st_absent "and no longer writes anything into the shared .build/" \
    "$default_cmd" '$PWD/.build/'

  printf '\n==> A stale lock does not become a new way to be stuck\n'
  # Three ways a lock goes stale, each recoverable with no hand-edit. A killed run leaves a lock
  # whose pid is gone; a Mac that has wrapped its pid space leaves one whose pid now belongs to
  # something else; and a run killed between the mkdir and the write leaves one with no owner
  # record at all.
  ( exit 0 ) &
  local dead_pid=$!
  wait "$dead_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$dead_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a battery behind a dead owner's lock still runs" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says it cleared a stale lock" "$ST_OUT" "cleared a stale lock"
  st_contains "and says which process is gone" "$ST_OUT" "pid $dead_pid) is gone"

  sleep 30 &
  local other_pid=$!
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$other_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a lock whose pid was reused is not treated as live" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and the reclaim says what the pid is now" "$ST_OUT" "belongs to something else now"
  kill "$other_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  wait "$other_pid" 2>/dev/null || true

  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a lock with no owner record is reclaimed" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says the record is missing" "$ST_OUT" "no owner record"

  printf '\n==> unlock clears what a killed run left, and reports an empty checkout honestly\n'
  st_run "$always_pass" unlock
  st_eq "unlock with no lock exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says there is nothing held" "$ST_OUT" "no run lock is held"
  ( exit 0 ) &
  dead_pid=$!
  wait "$dead_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
  mkdir -p .git/mutate.lock
  printf 'pid %s\nstarted 2026-01-01 00:00:00\nplan gone.plan\nhead unknown\n' "$dead_pid" \
    >.git/mutate.lock/owner
  st_run "$always_pass" unlock
  st_eq "unlock on a stale lock exits 0" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and says what it cleared" "$ST_OUT" "cleared a stale lock"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "unlock left the stale lock in place"
  else
    st_pass "the stale lock is gone"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A second battery inside a live one is refused BY THE LOCK, not by the tree guard\n'
  # The stand-in suite re-enters this script from inside the first run — the one moment a second
  # invocation would really interleave with a battery. The exit code alone proves nothing here,
  # and that is the whole reason this group is shaped the way it is: a mutant is applied at that
  # moment, so the tree genuinely IS dirty, and with the lock deleted outright require_clean_tree
  # would still exit 1 on the nested run. It would do it while printing the mutated file and the
  # words "Commit or stash the paths above" — advice that, followed, commits a mutant. So the
  # assertions below are that the refusal carries the lock's wording and carries neither of the
  # dirty-tree guard's two sentences.
  cat >"$root/reenter.sh" <<REENTER
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Stands in for the suite. Re-enters the harness only while the mutant is in the file. The nested
# run gets a suite command of its own so that a broken lock cannot recurse: with the mutant already
# applied its plan matches nothing and its pre-flight refuses.
if grep -q 'hour < 13' sample.swift; then
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='printf "X  Test run started.\n"' "$SELF" "$root/kill.plan" \
    >"$root/nested.out" 2>&1
  printf '%s' \$? >"$root/nested.exit"
  MUTATE_TEST_CMD='printf "X  Test run started.\n"' "$SELF" unlock \
    >"$root/nested-unlock.out" 2>&1
  printf '%s' \$? >"$root/nested-unlock.exit"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then printf 'yes' >"$root/lock-survived"; else printf 'no' >"$root/lock-survived"; fi
  printf 'X  Test run started.\n'
  printf 'X  Test labelIsMorningBeforeNoon() failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n'
  printf 'X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.001 seconds with 1 issue.\n'
  exit 1
fi
printf 'X  Test run started.\n'
printf 'X  Test run with 2 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.001 seconds.\n'
REENTER
  chmod +x "$root/reenter.sh"
  st_run "$root/reenter.sh" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the outer battery is undisturbed and still kills its mutant" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "the outer battery reported its kill" "$ST_OUT" "KILLED by 1 test(s)"
  st_eq "the nested battery exits 1" "$(cat "$root/nested.exit" 2>/dev/null)" "1"
  local nested_out
  nested_out="$(cat "$root/nested.out" 2>/dev/null)"
  st_contains "the nested refusal names the conflict" "$nested_out" "another battery already holds"
  st_contains "and names the run holding it" "$nested_out" "pid     :"
  st_contains "and names the recovery" "$nested_out" "unlock"
  st_absent "the nested refusal is not the dirty-tree guard" "$nested_out" "working tree is not clean"
  st_absent "and never tells anyone to commit a mutant" "$nested_out" "Commit or stash"
  st_eq "the refused run did not delete the live lock" "$(cat "$root/lock-survived" 2>/dev/null)" "yes"
  st_eq "unlock refuses to break a live battery" "$(cat "$root/nested-unlock.exit" 2>/dev/null)" "1"
  st_contains "and says which process still holds it" \
    "$(cat "$root/nested-unlock.out" 2>/dev/null)" "still running a battery"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "the lock outlived the battery that took it"
  else
    st_pass "the lock is released when the battery ends"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after the nested attempt" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""

  printf '\n==> A replacement suite command may not quietly build into the shared .build/\n'
  st_run 'swift test --disable-sandbox' "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "a SwiftPM command with no scratch path exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "and says what is missing" "$ST_OUT" "names no scratch path"
  st_contains "and hands over the variable to use" "$ST_OUT" "MUTATE_SCRATCH"
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "the refused run left its lock behind"
  else
    st_pass "the refused run released its lock"
  fi
  st_run ": swift test --scratch-path \"\$MUTATE_SCRATCH\"
$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_eq "the same command carrying a scratch path runs" "$ST_EXIT" "0"
  st_contains "and the report says the build directory is this run's own" "$ST_OUT" "this run's own"
  st_run "$kills_the_mutant" "$root/kill.plan"
  st_contains "a stand-in that is not SwiftPM is reported as having nothing to isolate" \
    "$ST_OUT" "no build directory to share"

  printf '\n==> An interrupted battery restores the mutant instead of leaving it in the tree\n'
  # The stand-in suite signals the harness while the mutant is applied — the one moment when an
  # abandoned run would leave mutated source on disk for someone to commit.
  st_run "$signals_the_harness" "$root/kill.plan"
  if [[ $ST_EXIT -ne 0 ]]; then
    st_pass "an interrupted battery exits non-zero (got $ST_EXIT)"
  else
    st_fail "an interrupted battery exited 0"
  fi
  st_contains "the battery had reached the mutant" "$ST_OUT" "the morning boundary widened"
  st_contains "it says what it restored" "$ST_OUT" "interrupted mid-mutant"
  if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
    st_fail "the mutant was left in the working tree"
  else
    st_pass "the mutant is gone from the working tree"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after an interrupt" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "an interrupted battery left its lock behind — every later run would need a reclaim"
  else
    st_pass "an interrupted battery released its lock"
  fi

  printf '\n==> A mutant file git does not track is refused, wherever it points\n'
  # `>>> file ../outside.txt` used to pass pre-flight and be mutated, with "Summary at <sha>"
  # printed over a file that SHA does not describe. A gitignored path had the same hole.
  # (PR #70 review, F2.)
  printf 'outside the repository\n' >"$root/outside.txt"
  printf 'ignored inside the repository\n' >"$root/repo/ignored.txt"
  printf 'ignored.txt\n' >>"$root/repo/.gitignore"
  git add .gitignore
  git commit -q -m "ignore ignored.txt"
  for target in "../outside.txt" "ignored.txt"; do
    cat >"$root/untracked.plan" <<PLAN
>>> mutant M1 a mutant aimed at a file git does not track
>>> file $target
>>> from
outside the repository
>>> to
mutated
>>> end
PLAN
    rm -f "$sentinel"
    st_run "$records_that_it_ran" "$root/untracked.plan"
    st_eq "a mutant aimed at $target exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
    st_contains "$target is reported as untracked" "$ST_OUT" "NOT TRACKED BY GIT"
    if [[ -e "$sentinel" ]]; then
      st_fail "a suite ran against $target"
    else
      st_pass "no suite ran for $target"
    fi
  done
  if grep -q "outside the repository" "$root/outside.txt"; then
    st_pass "the file outside the repository was never touched"
  else
    st_fail "a file outside the repository was mutated"
  fi

  printf '\n==> An internal error exits non-zero, and restores the mutant on the way out\n'
  # Two bugs are pinned here. On bash 3.2 the presence of an EXIT trap makes a `set -u` death exit
  # 0, so the harness printed a normal-looking report, died, and returned success (PR #70 review,
  # F6). And the EXIT trap must run `cleanup` BEFORE `assert_intended_exit`, or the exit ends the
  # trap with the mutant still in the tree — which nothing held until the seam was moved to fire
  # with a mutant applied (cycle-3 re-check, G1). The last three checks are the ordering-sensitive
  # ones: reverse the trap and they fail.
  ST_EXIT=0
  ST_OUT="$(MUTATE_TEST_CMD="$always_pass" MUTATE_INJECT_INTERNAL_ERROR=1 "$SELF" "$root/kill.plan" 2>&1)" || ST_EXIT=$?
  st_eq "a set -u death inside the harness exits 1" "$ST_EXIT" "1"
  st_contains "bash reports the unbound variable" "$ST_OUT" "unbound variable"
  st_contains "and the harness disowns the output above it" "$ST_OUT" "not one of its exits"
  st_contains "the death landed with a mutant applied" "$ST_OUT" "death with the mutant applied"
  st_contains "cleanup ran before the exit, and said so" "$ST_OUT" "interrupted mid-mutant"
  if grep -q "hour < 13" sample.swift; then
    st_fail "the mutant was left in the tree — cleanup did not run before assert_intended_exit"
  else
    st_pass "the mutant is gone from the working tree"
  fi
  st_eq "the tree is clean after an internal error" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""
  if [[ -d .git/mutate.lock ]]; then
    st_fail "a harness death left its lock behind"
  else
    st_pass "a harness death released its lock"
  fi

  printf '\n==> The file is restored byte for byte and the tree is left clean\n'
  if cmp -s sample.swift "$root/sample.pristine"; then
    st_pass "sample.swift is byte-identical to the committed version"
  else
    st_fail "sample.swift was not restored byte for byte"
  fi
  st_eq "git status --porcelain is empty" "$(git status --porcelain)" ""

  printf '\n'
  if [[ $SELFTEST_FAILURES -eq 0 ]]; then
    printf '==> selftest: all checks passed.\n'
    finish 0
  fi
  printf '==> selftest: %d check(s) FAILED.\n' "$SELFTEST_FAILURES" >&2
  finish 1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

main() {
  local plan="" only="" check_only="" arg

  case "${1:-}" in
    -h|--help|help)
      usage
      finish 0
      ;;
    selftest)
      [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || die "selftest takes no arguments"
      selftest
      ;;
    unlock)
      [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || die "unlock takes no arguments"
      unlock_command
      ;;
    "")
      usage >&2
      finish 1
      ;;
  esac

  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    arg="$1"
    case "$arg" in
      --only)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "--only needs a comma-separated list of mutant ids"
        only="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
      --check)
        check_only="yes"
        shift
        ;;
      -*)
        die "unknown option: $arg"
        ;;
      *)
        [[ -z "$plan" ]] || die "expected one plan file, got two: $plan and $arg"
        plan="$arg"
        shift
        ;;
    esac
  done

  [[ -n "$plan" ]] || die "no plan file given — scripts/mutate --help"
  # Resolved before battery() changes directory to the repository root.
  [[ "$plan" == /* ]] || plan="$PWD/$plan"

  WORKDIR="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/mutate.XXXXXX")"
  # Called plainly, never as `battery ... || rc=$?`: bash suspends errexit inside a command whose
  # status is being tested, so with that call shape a failing command *inside* battery — a `cp`
  # that cannot write, say — stops failing the run. Execution continues past it and the battery
  # reports a full summary built on a step that did not happen. Measured: with `|| rc=$?` restored
  # and a failing cp injected, the run completes and exits 2; called plainly it dies at exit 1 with
  # no summary.
  #
  # What this does NOT govern is a `set -u` death exiting 0 — that is the EXIT trap, and the
  # sentinel at the top of this file is what fixes it. This comment previously claimed otherwise
  # and the claim was wrong in a way the selftest could not see (PR #70 review, F6).
  battery "$plan" "$only" "$check_only"
}

main "$@"
